# The input program of section 4.1, in the absolute form it must have on the
# machine (it cannot load itself).
#
# Group ρ is not Böhm's. The thesis has no way to put constants in memory, so
# this follows Peter Sestoft's appendix A.2: ρ reads pairs (address, number)
# from the tape and stores them, until the address is 0; the number after
# that 0 is the address of α.
#
# Two things differ from the printed text. Böhm's a, b, c, d, f are written
# a0, b0, c0, d0, f0, so that loading a program does not disturb its letters.
# And the loader sits at 940, not 970, to make room for ρ and the constants.

origin 940
cells 970
temps 990

π' → ρ
? → Ω
? → ↓Ω
ρ → π

# α. The next instruction number on the tape is called a; if it is zero, stop.
π' → α
? → a0
(1 ∸ a0)·Ω + (1 ∩ a0)·β → π

# β. If the 5th to 10th digits of a are 050999 continue at γ, else at δ.
π' → β
a0 mod 10000 → b0
(a0 ∸ b0) → c0
(c0 + 9490010000) → f0
f0 mod 10000000000 → d0
(1 ∸ d0)·γ + (1 ∩ d0)·δ → π

# γ. The number in π′ is transferred to the address designated by b.
π' → γ
π' → ↓b0
α → π

# δ. The number a is transferred to the address designated by π′.
π' → δ
a0 → ↓π'
(π' + 1) → π'
α → π
